  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King threw 6.2 innings of two-run baseball. The offense scraped two runs across nine innings and twelve strikeouts. That was the game.",
    "what_this_means": "The Padres couldn't do anything until they absolutely had to, and by then it was too late. They left runners on in three straight critical innings. The first two sandwiched around zero production, the sixth a final gasp with nobody coming home. King gave them every chance to stay close. Twelve strikeouts is not an offense. It's surrender dressed up as a game. They never led. They trailed from the fourth inning forward. This is what inconsistency looks like when the pitching cooperates. Wasted air, wasted opportunity, a game that was there and then wasn't. One run in the final two innings tells you everything. They had the window. The bats didn't walk through.",
    "what_to_watch": "Connor Prielipp arrives with a 5.34 ERA. The Twins are 13 games over .500. This is where the Padres answer whether yesterday was a lapse or the real shape of the lineup right now.",
